  "configuration_id": "config_001",
  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so there is zero prior information about task difficulty, the relative contribution of RNA vs. image modalities, or whether multiprofile/complex encoders add value. The single most important unknown is the performance floor of a simple, well-understood baseline. 2) What this tests: config_001 (rna_only, singleprofile, pca_n2000) is the simplest possible configuration—no image branch, no multiprofile aggregation, no learned encoder beyond PCA. It establishes a clean, fast, interpretable baseline accuracy/F1 against which every subsequent design choice (adding image, switching to scvi/scgpt, moving to multiprofile, adding fusion) can be measured. 3) Why it is the best first experiment: Under a limited budget, the first run should minimize confounds and maximize interpretability. A PCA-based RNA-only single-profile model trains quickly, has no pretraining or hyperparameter sensitivity, and gives an unambiguous reference point. If it performs well, it tells us the task is tractable and that added complexity must clear a meaningful bar; if it performs poorly, it signals that the representation or modality choice is the critical lever to explore next. Starting here avoids the risk of attributing performance to the wrong factor in a more complex configuration."
}

